
  [1m2. Own server — small API.[0m A Node or Python service with Postgres or SQLite, your own magic-link flow (generate token,
  email it, exchange for a session), and a thin REST API the page calls. The change log is written in an
  application-layer service function that every mutation must route through. Full control, no vendor, and the event
  pipeline for C is entirely yours to shape. Cost: you build and own auth, email sending, sessions, deployment, and
  migrations — mostly work that isn't tasks or notifications. The change log is enforced by convention rather than by
  the database, so a future direct-write path can silently skip it. Complexity: moderate-high.

  [1m3. Local-first with sync.[0m Each client holds the full task list locally and syncs through a shared log (CRDT or an
  event-sourced stream). Offline works, and "what changed since I last looked" — exactly what notifications need — falls
  out of the design for free, because the sync log [3mis[0m the change log. It's the most elegant fit for the end goal. Cost:
  it's by far the hardest of the three, conflict semantics are subtle, and magic-link identity sits awkwardly on top.
  Complexity: high. I'd only take this if offline is a hard requirement.

  [1mMy recommendation is 1.[0m It cuts the most work off A while putting the change log somewhere it can't be circumvented,
  and the thing you actually care about — C — stays cheap. Option 2 is the right call if vendor independence matters to
  you more than speed; say so and I'll design against it instead. Option 3 I'd discard unless offline is a genuine
  requirement.

  Two things to settle alongside this, since they belong in the design rather than after it.

